---
description: Test files commenting and testing conventions
globs: [
  "packages/ai-edu-backend/tests/**/*.test.ts",
  "packages/ai-edu-backend/tests/helpers/**/*.ts"
]
alwaysApply: true
---
- Dont write comments if function/method name and params already reflect the intent.
- Test code should be lean and easy to read; avoid noisy or obvious comments.
- Remove redundant comments that restate what code already shows:
  - Setup/initialization: "Setup runs once", "Get school", "Get or create classroom" - obvious from code.
  - Operations: "Create one record", "Update the student", "Delete the student" - obvious from method calls.
  - Verifications: "Verify filtering works", "Should return 404" - obvious from assertions.
  - Flow: "First, set some categories", "Then update" - obvious from code order.
- Only add comments when explaining WHY (business logic, test isolation, edge cases, complex setup).
- Keep comments that explain test isolation strategies, edge cases, or non-obvious behavior.
- Use `Deno.test.beforeAll()` for shared setup (call `setupOnce()` once).
- Prefer `Deno.test()` with `t.step()` to group related scenarios in integration tests.
- Use descriptive test names: "Feature Name Integration Tests" or "Feature - Specific Scenario".
- Use module-scoped variables for shared clients.
- Follow Given-When-Then structure within test steps.
- When writing test code, read and follow `docs/res/testing/guide.md` for architecture and patterns.
- Test file naming: use `*.test.ts` suffix and name files after the feature being tested.
- Test names format: "Feature Name Integration Tests" or "Feature - Specific Scenario".
- Step names format: "Role/Subject can/cannot perform action" or another clear scenario description.
- When running any test command, prefix it with `NODE_ENV=test` (for example: `NODE_ENV=test deno test ...`).